.TH SDRFS 4
.SH NAME
sdrfs \- software-defined radio receiver as a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sdrfs
[
.B -S
] [
.B -l
.I seconds
] [
.B -m
.I mountpoint
] [
.B -s
.I spec
] [
.B -t
.I ms
] [
.B -w
.I seconds
] [
.I command ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sdrfs
serves a software-defined radio receiver, an RTL-SDR (an RTL2832U and its
tuner, the common USB dongle), as files, mounted by default at
.BR /mnt/sdr .
The receiver is reached through the host's
.IR rtl_tcp ,
from librtlsdr, which
.I sdrfs
dials.
It serves the radio, not what the radio hears: a demodulator is a
program that reads
.BR iq .
The
.I command
words, if given, are written to
.B ctl
once the files are mounted.
.PP
The files are also posted as
.B #ssdr/fs
.RI ( post9p (2);
.B -s
names another spec,
.B -S
posts none), so a process that did not see the mount can mount them.
Not posted, sdrfs exits when the mount goes.
.PP
.I Rtl_tcp
serves one client at a time.
While another holds it, a dial is answered but not greeted, and after a
client leaves it takes five seconds to take the next.
.B -w
is how long a dial waits for the greeting (15 seconds) before it is
refused as busy.
So the connection, the source command's dial included, is held
.B -l
seconds (10) after the last reader of
.B iq
leaves or the last setting is sent, and a reader within that takes it
at once.
Hanging up on
.I rtl_tcp
while it is still applying settings has crashed it; holding avoids that
too.
.SH FILESYSTEM
.TF status
.TP
.B ctl
one command a line.
A write is checked whole first: if any line is refused, none is done.
A read gives every setting, in the form written, after the source if
there is one.
.RS
.TP
.BI source " address"
.I rtl_tcp
at
.IB tcp! host ! port\f1,\fP
.IB host ! port\f1,\fP
or
.I host
(port 1234);
.B localhost
is dialled as
.BR 127.0.0.1 ,
which needs no connection server.
It is dialled at once, to see that it is
.I rtl_tcp
and learn its tuner, and the write returns when it has been, or fails
with the reason; the previous source is gone either way.
.TP
.B stop
no source.
.TP
.BI freq " hertz"
the frequency;
.BR k ,
.B M
and
.B G
are taken
.RB ( 1090M ,
.BR 162.025M ).
.TP
.BI rate " samples"
samples a second: 225001 to 300000, or 900001 to 3200000, as librtlsdr
takes; 2.4M is the most a dongle gives reliably.
.TP
.BI enc " encoding"
the samples' encoding:
.BR cu8 ,
8-bit unsigned I then Q, as the RTL2832U gives them, the only one;
any other is refused.
It changes nothing, but every line of
.BR iq 's
header is a command
.B ctl
takes.
.TP
.BR gain " auto | \fIdB\fP"
automatic gain, or the tuner's gain nearest
.IR dB ,
once the tuner is known
.RB ( gains ).
.TP
.BI ppm " n"
the frequency correction, in parts a million.
.TP
.BR agc " on | off"
the RTL2832U's own gain control.
.TP
.BR direct " off | i | q"
direct sampling of the I or Q branch, for frequencies below the tuner's
(HF), on dongles wired for it.
.TP
.BR biastee " on | off"
5 V on the antenna, for a preamplifier, on dongles that have it.
.RE
.TP
.B status
one line, attr=value:
.B link=none
(no source),
.B idle
(a source, not held),
.BR opening ,
.BR open ,
.B closed
(the stream ended) or
.BR error ;
.BI source= address;
.BI tuner= name
and
.BI gains= n
once dialled;
.BI readers= n ,
the opens of
.B iq
reading;
.BI bytes= n
given to readers,
.BI dropped= n
lost by readers that fell behind, and
.BI skipped= n
not given to streams not yet begun as tuned before a setting took
(below), since start;
.BI error= reason
for the last that failed.
.TP
.B gains
the tuner's gains, dB, one a line.
.TP
.B iq
a header, then the samples, from the open on: 8-bit unsigned I then Q,
two bytes a sample, 4.8 MB a second at 2.4M.
The header is as
.IR audio (6)'s:
lines of text, each a command
.B ctl
takes, then an empty line, padded with blanks to a multiple of 4 bytes:
.RS
.IP
.EX
rate 2400000
enc cu8
freq 99500000
.EE
.RE
.IP
It comes in reads of its own, before any sample.
It is true for the whole stream: a write to
.B ctl
that changes the rate or the frequency ends every open's stream, once
what it has queued is read, with end of file;
an open after it has a header saying what the radio is tuned to
now, on the connection still held, so it costs no dial.
A program reading
.B iq
is told the rate, never given it apart; one that reads to the end of
file reads one tuning.
The other settings
.RB ( gain ,
.BR ppm ,
.BR agc ,
.BR direct ,
.BR biastee )
change nothing the header says, and end nothing.
Each read waits for more and gives as much as is queued, up to its
count, a whole number of samples when it can.
The first open dials
.I rtl_tcp
if the connection is not held, and sends every setting; it is hung up
.B -l
seconds after the last close, so the radio is read only while something
listens or has just.
.I Rtl_tcp
answers no command and applies them one at a time, in 25 to 120
milliseconds each, so
.I sdrfs
reckons 150 each: samples arriving before the last should be done, and
.B -t
milliseconds (300) more, were tuned before it took.
A stream starts tuned: one not yet begun is not given them.
One begun is given every sample, a gain changing in it.
A hang-up waits for them too.
Every open has its own queue; one that falls more than 8 MB behind
loses the oldest, whole samples, counted in
.BR status .
When the stream ends (the dongle unplugged, say), or the source changes, a read gives what was
queued, then end of file (or the reason).
.SH CAPABILITY MODEL
One radio is one tuner on one frequency.
Every reader of
.B iq
hears what it is tuned to, and a setting written to
.B ctl
moves them all; a new rate or frequency ends their streams, as their
headers would no longer be true.
Tuning and listening are separate files, so what a process may do is
what is bound in its name space: a process given
.B iq
can listen to what the radio is tuned to; one given
.B ctl
chooses.
The receiver cannot transmit.
.PP
.I Rtl_tcp
has no authentication.
Keep it on the loopback address
.RB ( "rtl_tcp -a 127.0.0.1" )
and reach a radio on another machine by exporting
.B /mnt/sdr
from there over authenticated 9P, never by opening its port.
.PP
What may be listened to is law, which differs by country; nothing here
enforces it.
.SH EXAMPLES
On the host with the dongle:
.IP
.EX
$ rtl_tcp -a 127.0.0.1
.EE
.PP
In InferNode:
.IP
.EX
; sdrfs source localhost
; cat /mnt/sdr/status
link=open source=tcp!127.0.0.1!1234 tuner=R828D gains=29 readers=0 bytes=0 dropped=0 skipped=0
; echo 'freq 1090M
rate 2M
gain 49.6' > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; cat /mnt/sdr/iq > /tmp/adsb.iq	# until it is retuned
.EE
.PP
The recording says what it was tuned to, and tunes the radio back:
.IP
.EX
; sdr/iqhdr < /tmp/adsb.iq
rate 2000000
enc cu8
freq 1090000000
; sdr/iqhdr < /tmp/adsb.iq > /mnt/sdr/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sdrfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sdr (1),
